// @run
// @log z
// @hint redundant type annotation: `o` is inferred as `{v: {a: i32, b: string}[]}`
// A generic application as the ELEMENT of an inline-object field's list type
// (`{ v: Pair<i32, string>[] }`). The emitter's inline-shape interner splits the
// field list at its top-level commas, and its depth alphabet was `{}` `[]` `()`
// with no `<>` — so the argument list's own comma cut the FIELD list, the second
// half (`string>[]`) had no `:`, and `internInlineShape` refused the whole shape.
// Nothing downstream knew why: the annotation reached the type section as a bare
// "ref valtype with no interned shape".
//
// With the brackets counted (`tyGtIsClose`, D-GENBRACKET) the shape interned with
// its one code-5 ref-list field, and the reject moved to the gap that was actually
// left: the element `Pair<i32, string>` is a generic APPLICATION, and no interner
// reached it — `collectGenAliasShapes` only sees an application a TypeRef spells
// BARE (behind `| null` / `[]`), and this one is buried INSIDE the shape's field
// TEXT. So the element stayed un-interned and the code-5 field lookup failed
// loudly ("ref-list field element type is not interned"), while the named
// (non-generic) element alias — `type PairIS = {a: i32, b: string};
// { v: PairIS[] }` — compiled and ran.
//
// D-GAELEAF closes it: `internShapeDeep`, which already descends every wrapper
// (grouping / `| null` / `[]`) and a MAP's value position, now interns a
// generic-application LEAF through `gaeEnsure` — the shape-descent dual of the
// map-value `gaeEnsure` `collectGenAliasShapes` had for exactly this reason.
// Siblings that graduate with it: the same annotation in RETURN position
// (`type-arg-list-field-return.vl`) and a NESTED-array element
// (`type-arg-nested-list-field.vl`).
type Pair<A, B> = { a: A, b: B }

const o: { v: Pair<i32, string>[] } = { v: [{ a: 1, b: "z" }] }
print(o.v[0].b)
